{"idea_id":"IDEA-L24","title":"Recoding event support versus retaining predictive state","status":"partial","description":"Ask separately which state recodings make event updates coordinate-local and which observations retain exact first-order dynamics.","formulation":"docs/xor-mask-recoding.md defines binary-linear recoding of XOR directions, its basis criterion, a fixed-projection closure criterion and two stronger support obstructions.","expressive":"An invertible recoding preserves state information but may change event kinds and original relational meaning. A noninjective projection can lose consequential hidden rate dependence without adding a new primitive.","feasibility":"xor-mask-analysis-0.1.0 is an analysis-only standard-library checker. Exact proofs and bounded software fixtures are available; no new scientific worker or atlas round.","methods":"EQ-034, EQ-035 and RRC-I02 through RRC-I04. Binary rank versus subset-XOR; constructed bijections; exact block-row sums versus proportionality; parity and common-neighbor controls.","phenomena":"Common scaling cancels under normalisation; relative-rate changes can prevent closure; a frozen hidden coordinate can still matter for prediction.","alternatives":"Nonlinear recodings, changing clocks, latent-state models and evolving omitted coordinates are not classified. A failed old recoding is not proof of a new primitive.","related":["IDEA-L13","IDEA-L22","IDEA-E02","IDEA-E03"],"related_status":"conjectural"}
{"idea_id":"IDEA-L25","title":"Typed synchronization and context-preserving effective descriptions","status":"partial","description":"Study a composition with its own dynamical output type and distinguish standalone prediction from substitutability in an interacting system.","formulation":"docs/labelled-sync-composition.md declares the shared/private weighted-label product, iteration, identity, and a sufficient product-quotient theorem.","expressive":"The output is another weighted labelled component, allowing further composition. Labels and raw calibration can carry information absent from the standalone aggregate kernel; this does not establish new ontology.","feasibility":"label-sync-analysis-0.1.0 gives standard-library exact checks with software caps. Matrix proofs cover the specified finite domain; no new scientific worker.","methods":"EQ-036, EQ-037 and RRC-I05 through RRC-I07; sparse-event versus dense-matrix oracle, Fraction block sums and hand-derived contextual witnesses.","phenomena":"Hidden label choice can block joint motion; a private clock can reveal hidden scaling; weighted block sums can remain exact with evolving hidden state.","alternatives":"Other handshake or rate policies, changing interface membership, state-interface identification and weaker context-specific equivalences remain open.","related":["IDEA-L24","IDEA-L13","IDEA-L22"],"related_status":"conjectural"}
{"idea_id":"IDEA-L26","title":"Uniform context preservation and finite separating partners","status":"partial","description":"Distinguish safety with one partner from safety with every partner in an explicitly declared class; ask what finite observations can separate failures.","formulation":"docs/contextual-preservation.md gives an iff for the fixed-H product, calibrated finite probes, and a private-label relabelling congruence.","expressive":"A state-only observer can forget private label allocation but must generally retain private event weight and shared-label block weights. The role of a distinction depends on the interface, clock and allowed contexts.","feasibility":"context-probe-analysis-0.1.0 is analysis-only; finite proofs and independent exact signature/probability checks, no new scientific worker or engine semantics.","methods":"EQ-038/EQ-039 and RRC-I08/RRC-I09. Raw event signatures versus dense matrices and Fraction block rows; calibrated reconstruction, sparse compatibility and boundary controls.","phenomena":"Finite unit-calibrated partners expose hidden raw scale and shared-label allocation; private p/q allocation remains abstractable while H stays fixed and labels stay unseen.","alternatives":"Restricted partners, evolving interfaces or H, alternate composition policies, label-aware observation and general testing equivalences remain unclassified. No requirement to choose any as the next experiment.","related":["IDEA-L25","IDEA-L24","IDEA-L13"],"related_status":"conjectural"}
{"idea_id":"IDEA-L27","title":"Changing shared port values and boundary-preserving descriptions","status":"partial","description":"Compose finite systems by genuinely identifying a port value already read from constituent state, and ask which transition/abstraction contracts preserve later participation.","formulation":"docs/shared-changing-port.md defines the compatible-state product, private-write contract, inherited boundary, associativity/unit, agreement-invariance criterion and boundary-factorization lemma.","expressive":"The port can change and control rates without being additional independent state. The constructed example reduces to three ordinary single-toggle coordinates, while enforcing incompatible copy agreement is a declared transition constraint.","feasibility":"shared-port-analysis-0.1.0 is analysis-only with exact proofs, sparse/dense image oracles and bounded software controls. No new production worker or frozen round.","methods":"EQ-040 through EQ-043 and RRC-I10 through RRC-I12; support versus Fraction leakage, guarded matrices, bit-table reduction and predictive/type contrasts.","phenomena":"Equal port marginals do not create pathwise agreement; conditioning and rejected-attempt holds are different policies; a closed observation can lose the original boundary.","alternatives":"Independent membership/controller state, changing H, different private-write ownership, partial boundary types, typed minimal abstractions and other gluing policies remain open.","related":["IDEA-L25","IDEA-L26","IDEA-L22","IDEA-L13"],"related_status":"conjectural"}
{"idea_id":"IDEA-L28","title":"Switching attachment, fixed protocols and mode information","status":"partial","description":"Declare an independently changing relationship controlling local versus joint updates, and separate its exact finite-state encoding from predictive and primitive-support requirements.","formulation":"docs/switching-attachment.md defines a two-actor switching relation, a fixed-H controller compiler, the coarsest actor-view refinement and a positive binary support obstruction.","expressive":"The mode was specified as independent input state. Fixed shared labels plus passive participation reproduce the full raw dynamics, while the resulting joint-event support need not fit the single-toggle grammar under injective same-clock encodings.","feasibility":"switching-link-analysis-0.1.0 is analysis-only; proofs and independent guarded/event/refinement/support checks use small exact matrices. No production engine change or scientific worker.","methods":"EQ-044/EQ-045 and RRC-I13 through RRC-I15; 729 compiler cases, 11664 exhaustive refinement checks, hand rows, a three-state actor, old sparse composition and support-colouring controls.","phenomena":"Mode information is necessary in active actor configurations, dispensable for this prediction in inactive ones, and can still matter in later contexts. Fixed-mode encodings need not extend through switching.","alternatives":"Other attachment graphs, self-loop actor grammars, ownership/handoff rules, different coupling clocks, history observations and other relation types remain open; none is prescribed next.","related":["IDEA-L27","IDEA-L26","IDEA-L22","IDEA-L24"],"related_status":"conjectural"}

