{"from_finding":"RRC-ATLAS-013","rationale":"Atlas-009 stored distinct factor-2 tables that share a stationary mean. Atlas-012 stores three factor-3 singles that share mean edges 13/8. Two of them are the path reversal of one table. The third matches that table only after a bit swap that is not a carrier automorphism. The shared mean is not evidence that the atlas-009 marginals have the same cause. The factor-2 path grid was not rerun. The contrast does not explain the atlas-009 cells and does not close ATLAS-Q01 or ATLAS-Q03.","relation_id":"RRC-R047","relation_type":"contrasts_with","to_finding":"RRC-ATLAS-010"}
{"from_finding":"RRC-ATLAS-014","rationale":"Atlas-006 stored factor-3 eq-1 and ge-2 multiplies on five other carriers. Atlas-013 stores prohibits of those thresholds, and of eq 2, on one star. On that star eq 2 and ge 2 are the same table because the neighbourhood size is 2. The qualification is the effect and the neighbourhood bound. It does not explain the atlas-006 cells and does not close ATLAS-Q02 or ATLAS-Q09.","relation_id":"RRC-R048","relation_type":"qualifies","to_finding":"RRC-ATLAS-008"}
{"from_finding":"RRC-ATLAS-015","rationale":"Atlas-008 stored an exact degree sequence on the baseline four-cycle and an inexact position-indexed line graph. Atlas-014 uses the same carrier. The empty list repeats the four-edge constant-1 table. The prohibit makes the degree sequence, the edge count, and the line graph inexact. The qualification is the law. It does not explain the atlas-008 baseline defects and does not close ATLAS-Q13.","relation_id":"RRC-R049","relation_type":"qualifies","to_finding":"RRC-ATLAS-009"}
{"from_finding":"RRC-ATLAS-016","rationale":"Atlas-012 stored factor-3 multiplies on this three-edge chain and no block family. Atlas-015 uses the same carrier and the same empty-list table. Its four constrained lists are not those multiplies. The overlap maps are observations atlas-012 did not store. The qualification is the law and the observation. It does not explain the atlas-012 means and does not close ATLAS-Q03 or ATLAS-Q14.","relation_id":"RRC-R050","relation_type":"qualifies","to_finding":"RRC-ATLAS-013"}
{"from_finding":"RRC-ATLAS-016","rationale":"Atlas-011 stored fixed-cover maps on a triangle and a four-cycle under one multiply. Atlas-015 stores potential and realised maps of one chain cover under an empty list, two prohibits, and two other multiplies. The qualification is the carrier, the effect, and the realised adjacency. It does not explain the atlas-011 defects and does not close ATLAS-Q10 or ATLAS-Q14.","relation_id":"RRC-R051","relation_type":"qualifies","to_finding":"RRC-ATLAS-012"}
{"from_finding":"RRC-ATLAS-017","rationale":"Atlas-015 stored a prohibit of (1,2) when (0,1) is present and a multiply of (2,3) by 3 when (0,1) is present on the three-edge chain. Atlas-016 stores the same bit positions on edges (0,1), (1,2), and (3,4). The matrices match and the edge lists differ. The qualification is the carrier. It does not explain the atlas-015 overlap defects and does not close ATLAS-Q14 or ATLAS-Q02.","relation_id":"RRC-R052","relation_type":"qualifies","to_finding":"RRC-ATLAS-016"}
{"from_finding":"RRC-ATLAS-017","rationale":"Atlas-012 stored a multiply of (0,1) when (2,3) is present. Atlas-016 stores the same bit positions, with unread edge (1,2) and condition edge (3,4). The matrices match and the edge lists differ. The component means differ because the vertex counts differ. The qualification is the carrier. It does not close ATLAS-Q02 or ATLAS-Q03.","relation_id":"RRC-R053","relation_type":"qualifies","to_finding":"RRC-ATLAS-013"}
{"from_finding":"RRC-ATLAS-018","rationale":"The opening model does not add a dynamical primitive to the single-toggle grammar. Atlas-017 stores six laws under method event-kernel-0.1.0 and leaves the engine string rrc-0.4.0. The all-ones two-bit catalogue and the identified toggle stay inside one-step single-toggle support. The joint cell and the coupled cell put positive probability on a Hamming-distance-2 target. That is a support difference under the named clock, not a new primitive of rrc-0.4.0 and not a new coordinate. The qualification does not close ATLAS-Q06.","relation_id":"RRC-R054","relation_type":"qualifies","to_finding":"RRC-M01-NO-NEW-AXIS"}
{"from_finding":"RRC-ATLAS-019","rationale":"Atlas-017 matched a one-bit identified toggle with two joint-copy transitions on an agreement image. Atlas-018 checks the full three-bit to four-bit inclusion under a strict method, distinguishes invariant coupled copies from free-copy leakage, and leaves the older records and method unchanged. The larger image check qualifies the earlier local correspondence without closing ATLAS-Q10 or claiming a new primitive.","relation_id":"RRC-R055","relation_type":"qualifies","to_finding":"RRC-ATLAS-018"}
{"from_finding":"RRC-I01-INTERFACE-RECODING","rationale":"The proof and independent 256-entry recoding check use the verified stored coupled law. They are post-execution analysis, not extra frozen scientific items, and do not change the originally declared image or event kinds.","relation_id":"RRC-R056","relation_type":"derived_from","to_finding":"RRC-ATLAS-019"}
{"from_finding":"RRC-I01-INTERFACE-RECODING","rationale":"The named four-bit joint step is outside same-coordinate one-step single-toggle support. A declared XOR recoding nevertheless gives an ordinary three-toggle kernel with a frozen disagreement bit. This qualifies the encoding boundary; it does not claim every event law compiles or that the new basis preserves the original relational meaning.","relation_id":"RRC-R057","relation_type":"qualifies","to_finding":"RRC-M01-NO-NEW-AXIS"}
{"from_finding":"RRC-I02-XOR-SUPPORT-CLASSIFICATION","to_finding":"RRC-I01-INTERFACE-RECODING","relation_id":"RRC-R058","relation_type":"formalises","rationale":"EQ-033's three masks are independent. Basis extension explains the existence of that recoding while distinguishing its particular basis, the named-coordinate semantics and more general weight dependence."}
{"from_finding":"RRC-I03-PROJECTED-RATE-CLOSURE","to_finding":"RRC-I02-XOR-SUPPORT-CLASSIFICATION","relation_id":"RRC-R059","relation_type":"qualifies","rationale":"A recoding into coordinate updates constrains support, not whether hidden state can be discarded. Exact closure additionally requires equal normalised block laws, including the halt boundary; relative rate changes and common scaling give distinct cases."}
{"from_finding":"RRC-I04-CIRCUIT-SUPPORT-OBSTRUCTIONS","to_finding":"RRC-I02-XOR-SUPPORT-CLASSIFICATION","relation_id":"RRC-R060","relation_type":"bounds","rationale":"Linear dependence alone does not decide every nonlinear recoding. For two canonical dependent supports, triangle or common-neighbor invariants separately rule out the broader class of injective one-bit embeddings at the same clock. No general nonlinear classification is claimed."}
{"from_finding":"RRC-I03-PROJECTED-RATE-CLOSURE","to_finding":"RRC-I01-INTERFACE-RECODING","relation_id":"RRC-R061","relation_type":"qualifies","rationale":"The stored Atlas-018 law has identical uniform active rates in both disagreement sectors and therefore an exact quotient. That conclusion does not extend to arbitrary hidden-coordinate-dependent rates merely because the support remains recodable."}
{"relation_id":"RRC-R062","from_finding":"RRC-I05-SYNC-COMPOSITION-ITERATION","to_finding":"RRC-I06-SYNC-QUOTIENT-PRESERVATION","relation_type":"enables","rationale":"The explicit weighted-label output type and fixed handshake alphabet make reduction before or after an iterated dynamical composition a well-defined comparison."}
{"relation_id":"RRC-R063","from_finding":"RRC-I06-SYNC-QUOTIENT-PRESERVATION","to_finding":"RRC-I07-AGGREGATE-CONTEXT-LOSS","relation_type":"bounds","rationale":"Labelwise raw block-weight equality is a sufficient interface contract preventing the demonstrated contextual loss, while a particular shared-only partner need not require that full condition."}
{"relation_id":"RRC-R064","from_finding":"RRC-I07-AGGREGATE-CONTEXT-LOSS","to_finding":"RRC-I03-PROJECTED-RATE-CLOSURE","relation_type":"qualifies","rationale":"Common scaling cancels in the earlier standalone normalised projection but can become visible relative to a partner's private event. The old standalone result remains valid within its declared scope."}
{"relation_id":"RRC-R065","from_finding":"RRC-I07-AGGREGATE-CONTEXT-LOSS","to_finding":"RRC-ATLAS-018","relation_type":"qualifies","rationale":"The frozen halt/noop kernel equality does not imply substitutability as a synchronizing component: the one-state identity offers shared events whereas empty-row completion offers none. No frozen atlas claim is changed."}
{"relation_id":"RRC-R066","from_finding":"RRC-I08-UNIFORM-CONTEXT-CRITERION","to_finding":"RRC-I06-SYNC-QUOTIENT-PRESERVATION","relation_type":"qualifies","rationale":"For the declared rich context class, a necessary-and-sufficient condition uses separate shared block sums but only the aggregate private sum. The earlier full-label sufficient theorem remains valid and is not silently rewritten."}
{"relation_id":"RRC-R067","from_finding":"RRC-I09-PRIVATE-LABEL-ABSTRACTION","to_finding":"RRC-I08-UNIFORM-CONTEXT-CRITERION","relation_type":"enables","rationale":"Collapsing private names gives the exact weighted block contract needed by the state-only context class, allowing a weaker contract than preservation of every original label."}
{"relation_id":"RRC-R068","from_finding":"RRC-I08-UNIFORM-CONTEXT-CRITERION","to_finding":"RRC-I07-AGGREGATE-CONTEXT-LOSS","relation_type":"formalises","rationale":"Calibrated separating contexts characterize which hidden labels or scales can defeat closure under this product, rather than generalising from the earlier two selected partners."}
{"relation_id":"RRC-R069","from_finding":"RRC-I09-PRIVATE-LABEL-ABSTRACTION","to_finding":"RRC-I06-SYNC-QUOTIENT-PRESERVATION","relation_type":"qualifies","rationale":"A component with hidden private p/q allocation violates the old full-label condition yet is safe in every fixed-H state-only context. Changing H or the observer invalidates that abstraction."}
{"relation_id":"RRC-R070","from_finding":"RRC-I08-UNIFORM-CONTEXT-CRITERION","to_finding":"RRC-I03-PROJECTED-RATE-CLOSURE","relation_type":"qualifies","rationale":"Standalone common-scale cancellation does not remove raw calibration from the rich-context contract. A unit competing private event exposes raw weight, including internal-block events, under the same declared product clock."}
{"relation_id":"RRC-R071","from_finding":"RRC-I10-SHARED-PORT-COMPOSITION","to_finding":"RRC-I05-SYNC-COMPOSITION-ITERATION","relation_type":"qualifies","rationale":"Identifying an onto common port and filtering incompatible targets defines a new interaction operator. Its inherited boundary and raw dynamical associativity require their own proof, not a silent reuse of the Cartesian operation."}
{"relation_id":"RRC-R072","from_finding":"RRC-I11-SHARED-PORT-IMAGE","to_finding":"RRC-I10-SHARED-PORT-COMPOSITION","relation_type":"qualifies","rationale":"Well-typed identified composition need not be an invariant restriction of unconstrained copies. Target consistency exactly separates unchanged image dynamics from filtering and renormalisation."}
{"relation_id":"RRC-R073","from_finding":"RRC-I12-PREDICTION-PORT-TYPE-BOUNDARY","to_finding":"RRC-I08-UNIFORM-CONTEXT-CRITERION","relation_type":"qualifies","rationale":"The previous all-partner theorem concerns unconstrained Cartesian states. An identified-port context also requires recovery of the original boundary from a replacement observation, which standalone predictive closure does not supply."}
{"relation_id":"RRC-R074","from_finding":"RRC-I12-PREDICTION-PORT-TYPE-BOUNDARY","to_finding":"RRC-M01-NO-NEW-AXIS","relation_type":"formalises","rationale":"The specified changing-port feedback law is exactly three current-state coordinate flips with one conditional factor-2 multiply. Dynamic interface language does not make this constructed finite law a new single-toggle primitive."}
{"relation_id":"RRC-R075","from_finding":"RRC-I10-SHARED-PORT-COMPOSITION","to_finding":"RRC-I12-PREDICTION-PORT-TYPE-BOUNDARY","relation_type":"enables","rationale":"The composite retains an explicit boundary for later participation, making inherited-port factorization a precise requirement separate from obtaining a closed observed Markov kernel."}
{"relation_id":"RRC-R076","from_finding":"RRC-I13-SWITCHING-LINK-PROTOCOL","to_finding":"RRC-I10-SHARED-PORT-COMPOSITION","relation_type":"qualifies","rationale":"The new input relation changes whether actors update separately or together, while the previous interface kept its participation/type fixed and changed only a shared value. The new policy is independently specified and exactly reduced to a guarded protocol."}
{"relation_id":"RRC-R077","from_finding":"RRC-I14-LINK-MODE-MINIMAL-REFINEMENT","to_finding":"RRC-I12-PREDICTION-PORT-TYPE-BOUNDARY","relation_type":"qualifies","rationale":"This gives a proved coarsest actor-preserving current-state observation for a different switching-link grammar. It does not turn prediction minimality into an inherited-boundary or all-context substitutability theorem."}
{"relation_id":"RRC-R078","from_finding":"RRC-I15-SWITCHING-LINK-SUPPORT-BOUNDARY","to_finding":"RRC-I13-SWITCHING-LINK-PROTOCOL","relation_type":"qualifies","rationale":"The exact finite-state fixed-protocol reduction retains joint updates. A positive switching support has an odd cycle and cannot also preserve one-bit event support under the specified injective same-clock encodings."}
{"relation_id":"RRC-R079","from_finding":"RRC-I13-SWITCHING-LINK-PROTOCOL","to_finding":"RRC-I14-LINK-MODE-MINIMAL-REFINEMENT","relation_type":"enables","rationale":"The explicit local/joint/switch event grammar and common global-event clock separate outgoing actor-block supports, making the refinement theorem well-defined and independently checkable."}
{"relation_id":"RRC-R080","from_finding":"RRC-I15-SWITCHING-LINK-SUPPORT-BOUNDARY","to_finding":"RRC-M01-NO-NEW-AXIS","relation_type":"qualifies","rationale":"Representation discipline requires distinguishing a finite-state guarded encoding from a compiler preserving the old single-toggle support. This scoped obstruction is not a new fundamental primitive or a universal impossibility of simulation."}

